+# GLM-5.3-Flash: the attention/expert split (`--experts remote`)
+
+`--experts remote` serves GLM-5.3-Flash's attention, KV cache, drafter and sampling on one CUDA GPU and puts the
+routed experts on two `tensorfold experts` nodes. The three machines form one three-rank group: the attention node
+is rank 0, and each expert node holds one two-way half of the routed experts as ranks 1 and 2. In every MoE layer the
+attention node sends the layer's rows, the router's picks and the routing weights to both expert nodes; each runs its
+half's experts and returns its partial sum, while the attention node runs the layer's shared expert; the three are
+added and the forward goes on.
+
+The exchange runs over Ash Hart's [MCDMA](https://github.com/ashhart/MCDMA) link daemons: per MoE layer one packed
+request into each expert node's mailbox and one reply back. It is GPU-driven where the device supports it (the
+mailbox words are written and awaited as stream memory operations on the serving stream, with no host sync per
+layer) and CPU-driven otherwise.
+
+The attention node holds only the non-expert weights, the KV cache and the drafter, so a 32 GB GPU carries a
+262,144-token window, and each expert node needs no HTTP, tokenizer or drafter: only its half of the experts.
+
+## The three nodes
+
+| Role | Command | Holds |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Attention (rank 0) | `tensorfold serve CKPT --experts remote --master <attention-ip>` | non-expert weights, the KV cache, the drafter; serves HTTP |
+| Expert half 0 (rank 1) | `tensorfold experts CKPT --rank 0 --master <attention-ip>` | its half of every MoE layer's routed experts |
+| Expert half 1 (rank 2) | `tensorfold experts CKPT --rank 1 --master <attention-ip>` | the other half |
+
+`CKPT` is the same EXL3 checkpoint on all three machines (the [CUDA recipe](glm-5.3-flash.md)); the attention node
+refuses other formats at load. `--master` is the attention node's address on the network the experts reach it by,
+and both commands default to `--master-port 29551`. The experts take the window and chunking from the attention
+node. `--experts remote` refuses `--tp`, because the expert nodes are the second and third ranks.
+
+Start the attention node first: it hosts the rendezvous and publishes the settings the experts must match. An expert
+whose checkpoint, wire or link map disagrees exits with a message naming both values. Each expert writes a loaded
+mark once its half is resident; then the attention's `/health` answers, and both experts keep beating every 5 s. 20 s
+of silence, a link failure or an expert error raises the attention's watchdog: in-flight requests fail with HTTP 503
+and the attention logs `afd: ...; in-flight requests fail, restart the serving stack`. Restart the whole stack, not
+one node.
+
+## The MCDMA wire
+
+Each host needs:
+
+- a `mcdma-rpcd` link daemon: the attention node is the connect end of both links, and each expert node is the
+  listen end of its own link (its socket is found through `MCDMA_RPCD_SOCKET`);
+- `TF_AFD_MCDMA_LIB` pointing at a `libmcdma-rpc.so` build (ABI 1) for the host's architecture; the loader also
+  probes `/mcdma/libmcdma-rpc.so` and `/usr/local/lib/libmcdma-rpc.so`;
+- link names: `TF_AFD_MCDMA_LINKS=<link-for-rank1>,<link-for-rank2>` on the attention node and
+  `TF_AFD_MCDMA_LINK=<name>` on each expert node.
+
+Mailboxes are created under `/dev/shm` (`TF_AFD_MCDMA_SHM_DIR`). `TF_AFD_MCDMA_MODE=auto` (the default) runs a
+capability probe and drives the exchange from the GPU when it passes, else from the CPU with a loud startup line;
+`gpu` and `cpu` force a mode. `tools/mcdma_streamops_probe.py` runs the same checks on a node before its first boot.
+A request may stay unanswered `TF_AFD_MCDMA_TIMEOUT` seconds (default 30) before the attention's watchdog calls the
+link dead. The startup line names the mode: `afd transport mcdma, gpu-driven` or `cpu-driven`.
+
+## Mia's AI Lab's GLM work on the split
+
+The split carries MiaAI-Lab's GLM work for TensorFold: her own pull requests, kept as she wrote them, and ports of her
+[GLM-5.3-Flash EXL3 2x DGX Sparks recipe](https://github.com/MiaAI-Lab/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold)
+patches. On the attention node they run at world 1, beside the wire:
+
+| Setting | What | From |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `--parallel N` (2 to 4) | several requests decode together: one cache pool, batched verify windows, DFlash2 drafts for every stream | #243 (MiaAI-Lab); the world-1 glue is the split's |
+| with `--parallel` | `/health`'s `iteration_s` and `TF_GLM_MULTI_WATCHDOG_EXIT`; its sealed rank messages are for two ranks (world 1 sends none) | #243 (MiaAI-Lab) |
+| (always) | a tool call the end token left open is still sent when it parses whole | #285 (MiaAI-Lab) |
+| (always) | a client that leaves, or a stop string, ends its run within a round | #301 (MiaAI-Lab) |
+| `TF_GLM_DENSE=q4` | dense weights in MSE-searched 4-bit groups of 64, the head in FP8 | recipe patches 0002, 0005, 0013, 0028 (ported) |
+| `TF_GLM_KV=fp8` | the DSA latent and indexer caches as e4m3 rows with a power-of-two scale each | recipe patch 0038 and 0041's ring span (ported) |
+
+Her recipe's defaults are her checkpoint, the DFlash2 drafter, 4-bit dense weights, the FP8 KV cache and four
+streams. On the split:
+
+```bash
+TF_GLM_DENSE=q4 TF_GLM_KV=fp8 \
+  tensorfold serve CKPT --experts remote --master <attention-ip> --drafter DRAFTER --context 262144 --parallel 4
+```
+
+The expert nodes take no part in any of these settings, and serve unchanged.
+
+Under `--parallel` the attention node verifies every stream's window in one eager forward over the wire: it captures
+no batched CUDA graphs (`TF_GLM_MULTI_GRAPHS` is ignored, and the startup line says so), and it refuses
+`TF_GLM_MULTI_DEPTH=joint` and `TF_GLM_MULTI_LONE=1`, which need them, before the wire opens. Each reply equals the
+one its request gets alone, as on two ranks. Without the flags, the attention node runs as described above.
+
+## Memory
+
+Attention node, 32 GB card (32,607 MiB), BF16 dense weights and BF16 caches at a 262,144-token window: non-expert
+weights 17.3 GiB, the DFlash2 drafter 0.9 GiB, caches and fixed buffers 10.2 GiB; the startup estimate asks 28.18 GiB.
+On a discrete card TensorFold's default reserve is the larger of 4 GiB and a tenth of the card, which leaves about
+26.7 GiB, so set `TENSORFOLD_MEMORY_RESERVE_GIB=2` (admission then measures 28.18 within a 28.65 GiB budget) or take
+a smaller window.
+
+With `TF_GLM_DENSE=q4` the weights are 10.4 GiB smaller, and `TF_GLM_KV=fp8` takes 6,716 bytes a window token
+instead of 18,112. Together the estimate at 262,144 is about 15.0 GiB with one stream and 16.1 GiB with
+`--parallel 4`. The streams share one cache pool, which `TF_GLM_CACHE_GIB` sizes (default 3 GiB, about 512K tokens
+with FP8 caches); four streams at full depth at once need a larger pool.
+
+Expert node: its half of the routed experts, 71.1 GiB, a 2,048-row chunk's scratch (about 1.9 GiB) and the CUDA
+context; measured peak allocation 72.4 GiB on one half. On each 128 GB unified-memory host, 28 to 31 GiB stayed
+available after load.
+
+## Limits
+
+- The `glm5_next` CUDA family and EXL3 checkpoints only; `tensorfold experts` refuses other families, and the
+  Mac/MLX lane is untouched.
+- Eager execution only: graphs.py captures nothing on the attention node. A captured exchange measured within 0.5%
+  of eager, so graph capture is not part of this change.
+- `--parallel` up to 4 requests; the 262,144-token window is the tested one, and the startup estimate admits what
+  fits.
+- The wire check (`TF_AFD_CHECK=1`) clones each layer's wire tensors (about 3.4 GB at a 2,048-row chunk), so on a
+  32 GB card a checked wide chunk fits only at a 98,304-token window or less. Serve 262,144 with the check off.
+- The MTP head does not cross the wire: the attention node does not load it, and `TF_GLM_MTP=1` is refused.
+  TensorFold's EXL3 auto policy drafts with DFlash2 anyway.
+- The recipe's FP8 dense weights (`TF_GLM_DENSE=fp8`) are not ported.
+
+## Troubleshooting
+
+- **Startup hangs at the handshake.** Order: the attention node first, then the experts in either order; the same
+  `--master-port` on all three; the attention waits for both loaded marks.
+- **`afd: ...` and 503s.** An expert died or its beats went stale (5 s beats, 20 s stale), or a link failed. The log
+  names the cause; restart the serving stack.
+- **MCDMA link loss.** A stopped or replaced daemon, or a hung expert, fails the forward with a `LinkError`; the
+  attention's watchdog calls a link dead after `TF_AFD_MCDMA_TIMEOUT` (30 s) of an unanswered request.
+- **CPU-driven when GPU-driven was expected.** Run `tools/mcdma_streamops_probe.py` on that node: it names the
+  missing device attribute or the failing stream memory operation.
+- **Building `libmcdma-rpc`.** At MCDMA e672c14, GCC 11.4 and 13.3 stop the rpc build on
+  `-Werror=format-truncation` (`rpcd_connect.c:42`); the open ashhart/MCDMA#5 fixes it.
+- **CUDA extensions.** Build them before serving, into a persistent `TORCH_EXTENSIONS_DIR`. Compiling inside
+  `serve`, after about 90 GiB of weights were resident on a stock two-rank node, drove a 128 GB host down to 5.7 GiB
+  available.
+- **Wire audit.** `TF_AFD_CHECK=1` hashes both sides' wire tensors every layer for `TF_AFD_CHECK_FORWARDS` forwards
+  (default 4096) and prints `afd-check: window complete, N forwards, M mismatches total, rows checked [...]`.
+  `TF_AFD_TIME=1` times the per-layer exchanges.
+
